[Event "Nimzo-Indian: Rubinstein 4.e3"]

{The Nimzo-Indian (3...Bb4) pins the c3-knight to stop e4. Black is willing to give up the bishop pair to double White's c-pawns.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
2. c4 {Space. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
2... e6 {Prepares ...Bb4. Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
3. Nc3 {Prepares e4. Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
3... Bb4 {[%cal Gb4c3] The Nimzo: pins the knight and stops e4. Develops the bishop to b4, where it controls c5 and d6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
4. e3 {The Rubinstein. Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 controls f4 and supports the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
4... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
 (4... c5 {Strikes d4 at once. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 supports the bishop on b4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
5. Bd3 {Develops the bishop to d3, where it controls e4.}
5... Nc6 {More pressure on d4. Develops the knight to c6, where it controls e5 and eyes the pawn on d4.}
6. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
6... Bxc3+ {Doubles the c-pawns. The bishop on c3 eyes the pawn on d4 and the pawn on b2. It is check, so the reply is forced.}
7. bxc3 {Recaptures toward the centre. Recaptures on c3, restoring the material balance.}
7... d6 {The Hübner plan: ...e5 and a dark-square blockade against the doubled pawns.}
)
5. Bd3 {Develops the bishop to d3, where it controls e4.}
5... d5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls e4 and eyes the pawn on c4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
6. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
6... c5 {Hits d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 supports the bishop on b4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
7. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
7... dxc4 {Takes the pawn on c4; White will want to recapture.}
8. Bxc4 {Recaptures on c4, restoring the material balance.}
8... Nc6 {Develops the knight to c6, where it controls e5 and eyes the pawn on d4.}
9. a3 {Asks the bishop. The pawn on a3 eyes the bishop on b4.}
9... Ba5 {Keeps the pin. Moves the bishop out of attack to a5.} *

[Event "Nimzo-Indian: Classical, Sämisch & Leningrad"]

{4.Qc2 avoids doubled pawns, 4.f3 prepares e4, 4.Bg5 pins.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
2. c4 {Space. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
2... e6 {Prepares ...Bb4. Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
3. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
3... Bb4 {Nimzo. Develops the bishop to b4, where it controls c5 and d6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
4. Qc2 {The Classical. The queen goes to c2 and controls e4.}
 (4. f3 {Prepares e4. Supports the centre: the pawn on f3 controls e4 and g4.}
4... d5 {Stops e4. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls e4 and eyes the pawn on c4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
5. a3 {Asks the bishop. The pawn on a3 eyes the bishop on b4. That bishop is currently undefended.}
5... Be7 {Keeps the bishop. Moves the bishop out of attack to e7. White's last move put a pawn on a3, which this now pressures.}
6. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls f5 and eyes the pawn on d5. It also opens a line for the bishop on f1.}
6... dxe4 {Takes the pawn on e4; White will want to recapture.}
7. fxe4 {Recaptures on e4, restoring the material balance.}
7... e5 {Strikes. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls f4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
8. d5 {Gains space. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c6 and e6.}
8... Bc5 {Active. The bishop goes to c5 and controls d4.}
)
 (4. Bg5 {The Leningrad. Develops the bishop to g5, where it controls f4 and eyes the knight on f6.}
4... h6 {Asks the bishop. The pawn on h6 eyes the bishop on g5. That bishop is currently undefended.}
5. Bh4 {Keeps the pin. Moves the bishop out of attack to h4.}
5... c5 {Hits d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 supports the bishop on b4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
6. d5 {Gains space. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c6 and eyes the pawn on e6.}
6... d6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls e5 and supports the pawn on c5.}
)
4... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
5. a3 {Asks the bishop. The pawn on a3 eyes the bishop on b4. That bishop is currently undefended.}
5... Bxc3+ {Takes. It is check, so the reply is forced. Takes the knight on c3; White will want to recapture. It gives check.}
6. Qxc3 {Recaptures on c3, restoring the material balance.}
6... b6 {Fianchetto. The pawn on b6 controls a5 and c5.}
7. Bg5 {Pins. Develops the bishop to g5, where it controls f4 and eyes the knight on f6.}
7... Bb7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on b7, where it rakes the long diagonal through c6, d5 and e4.}
8. f3 {Stops ...Ne4. Supports the centre: the pawn on f3 controls e4 and g4.}
8... h6 {Asks. The pawn on h6 eyes the bishop on g5. That bishop is currently undefended.}
9. Bh4 {Keeps the pin. Moves the bishop out of attack to h4.}
9... d5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls e4 and eyes the pawn on c4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.} *
